<!-- BELIEVE SECTION -->
      <h2 class="text-4xl pt-28 pb-10" style="font-weight: 400">My approach: </h2>
      <section class="grid md:grid-cols-3 pt-5 gap-16">
        <div>
            <h2 class="text-3xl text-[#241C15] pb-6" style="font-weight: 500">Build for people.</h2> 
            <p class="text-2lg">"User" removes the relationship from the interaction. 
                I design and build for real people and organizations, not "users." 
                 </p>
        </div>

        <div>
            <h2 class="text-3xl text-[#241C15] pb-6" style="font-weight: 500">Prioritize basics.</h2> 
            <p class="text-1xl">I grind the basics to make sure my knowledge base is strong. Plus, I like learning from 
                the past, especially if that method has withstood the test of time.  
            </p>
        </div>

        <div>
            <h2 class="text-3xl text-[#241C15] pb-6" style="font-weight: 500">Simplicity is quality.</h2> 
            <p class="text-1xl">To me, quality design and code is simple. It's fast. And I don't have time for the flashy stuff (that usually breaks fast anyway). </p>
        </div>

        <div>
            <h2 class="text-3xl text-[#241C15] pb-6" style="font-weight: 500">AI is not a crutch.</h2> 
            <p class="text-1xl">I don't rely on AI-generated code. At most, it's a brainstorming and organizational partner. 
                Pen and paper works just fine. 
            </p>
        </div>

        <div>
            <h2 class="text-3xl text-[#241C15] pb-6" style="font-weight: 500">Love what you do.</h2> 
            <p class="text-1xl">What's the point in doing it if you don't love it? When I was 10 years old, I learned to code 
                using pen and paper. I'm still here (and loving it). </p>
        </div>

        <div>
            <h2 class="text-3xl text-[#241C15] pb-6" style="font-weight: 500">Be open to learning.</h2> 
            <p class="text-1xl">Everyone and everything, even the smallest detail and newest person, has something valuable to teach. 
                Never be close-minded to miss those learning opportunities.  </p>
        </div>
        
      </section>